commit 068732e60d8a0c39f352c4e0f5069c54c3f33cda
Author: lora-dataset-studio <noreply@lora-dataset-studio.dev>
Date:   Sun Jul 26 10:10:08 2026 +0200

    feat(engines): OpenRouter as a third image engine — one key for the same models
    
    Generating a dataset required an account per provider: a Gemini key for Nano
    Banana, an OpenAI key (or a ChatGPT plan) for ChatGPT. People who already pay
    for OpenRouter — one account and one balance fronting most providers — had no
    way in at all. Requested by jqs (GitHub #13).
    
    OpenRouter's image API (POST /api/v1/images) serves the very models the two
    existing engines call directly: google/gemini-3-pro-image (Nano Banana's) and
    openai/gpt-image-2 (ChatGPT's), plus Seedream, FLUX and others, and it takes
    reference images through `input_references`. So this is not a lesser
    substitute — it changes who bills you, not what the pictures look like.
    
    Strictly additive. `nanobanana` and `chatgpt` behave exactly as before; the new
    engine id and its `ORFace` filename tag are appended, never a rename or a
    reorder, because both are persisted (dataset rows, filenames on disk).
    
    - services/openrouter.py: generate_variation() on the existing engine contract
      (refs + prompt + aspect_ratio -> bytes|None), OPENROUTER_API_KEY in
      SECRET_KEYS so it inherits presence-only exposure.
    - engines.openrouter_model, free text, default google/gemini-3-pro-image: the
      catalogue moves fast and a retired slug must never require a release.
      Nothing caps the reference count either — models accept 1 to 16 and that
      ceiling drifts, so every reference is sent and a refusal REPORTS the count
      instead of the app silently dropping some.
    - Fails loudly, always. No key / rejected key / no credits / unknown model /
      rate limit each get their own message carrying OpenRouter's own words, and
      none of them ever falls back to another engine — being billed somewhere you
      did not choose is worse than a failed row. The four causes that would refuse
      every remaining row identically stop the batch, reusing the machinery the
      ChatGPT quota stop already uses, instead of paying for the same refusal once
      per image. A None return is reserved for a genuine 200-without-image refusal.
    - The key is a secret end to end: Authorization header only, never in a log
      line, an exception, a diagnostic or the settings payload. Locked by tests.
    - UI: fourth engine card (fuchsia, naming the model it will bill), OpenRouter
      key + model fields in Settings and in the Setup wizard, capability probe,
      help-registry topics, What's new, settings-reference.
    
    NOT VERIFIED AGAINST THE REAL SERVICE. There is no OpenRouter key or credit
    here, so nothing in this change has ever exchanged a byte with OpenRouter: the
    request shape and the response parsing come from their published API docs, and
    every test stubs the transport. Whoever enables this engine first is the first
    to exercise it for real. That is precisely why every failure path is named and
    noisy rather than quietly degraded — a wrong assumption here should announce
    itself, not look like a bad prompt.
    
    Backend 2525 tests green, frontend 759 green.
